>
  <p>
    Before you hire anyone for
    <a href="/services/paid-ads">Google ads</a>,
    <a href="/services/local-seo">local SEO</a>, or a
    <a href="/services/websites">new website</a>, learn to spot the agencies that sound busy and
    still leave you broke. This is the first article we wish every new client read.
  </p>

  <h2>The bad-agency pattern</h2>
  <p>
    They sell <strong>leads</strong> as the win. Volume looks great in a slide deck. Your techs hate
    the calls. Nobody is measuring booked estimates on the calendar, and the agency gets paid either
    way.
  </p>
  <p>
    They over-promise timelines and under-explain how Google actually works. When results stall, they
    blame your industry, your prices, or “the algorithm,” instead of checking whether the
    <a href="/blog/google-category-relevance">Google Business Profile</a>, site, and ads even agree
    on what you do.
  </p>
  <p>
    They lock you into a site you don’t own, a dashboard you can’t interpret, and a retainer that
    goes up when nothing changes. Flashy rebuilds that kill organic are a classic, we’ve cleaned up
    after more than one.
  </p>

  <h2>What a good partner looks like</h2>
  <p>
    Shared goal: <strong>booked jobs</strong> and revenue that outruns spend, not a lead dump.
    Honest talk when a channel is soft. Willingness to fix the website before pouring ad budget on a
    page that doesn’t convert. Starting with cheaper proof (often Local Services Ads) before
    expensive Search scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    Humility when someone else might do a piece better, and competence when they don’t. Long
    relationships look boring from the outside: consistent calls, clear reporting, no fluff.
  </p>

  <h2>A quick sniff test</h2>
  <p>
    Ask: “How do you define a booked job?” “What happens in the first 30 days?” “What won’t you
    guarantee?” If the answers are vague, lead-obsessed, or full of magic numbers, keep shopping.
  </p>
</BlogLayout>
